<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n15501" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Ĕlissa</span></b>  or <b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Ĕlīsa</span></b> , ae,
		f., = <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Ἔλισσα</span>, another name
		(occurring only in poetry) for <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>Dido</i>, <i><span class="en">Verg. A. 4, 335</span></i>; <i><span class="en">610</span></i>;
				<i><span class="en">5, 3</span></i>; <i><span class="en">Ov. Am. 2, 18, 31</span></i>; <i><span class="en">id. F. 3,
				553 al.</span></i>—Hence, </div>
        <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>II.</b>
			<b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Ĕlis-saeus</span></b> 
			(Elīsaeus), a, um, adj., <b>poet.</b> for <i>Carthaginian</i>: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">patres,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">Sil.
					6, 346</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">tyrannus,</span>”
				i. e. <i>Hannibal</i>,
				 <i><span class="en">id. 2,
					239</span></i>
			: 
				“<span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">lacerti,</span>”
				 <i><span class="en">id.
					15, 524</span></i>
			.</div>
